ENTRY 034 - STANDING TUNING AUTHORITY; DEEP THINKING-CONFIG FIX
recorded (UTC): 2026-08-31T04:10:53.0659266Z
recorded by: builder
================================================================================

  "Fix the config and rerun in a course when you run into issues where you're just tuning,
   and it's going to be more efficient and make the whole system perform better. Absolutely
   go ahead and do it."

WHAT THIS AUTHORIZES

A standing grant: where the builder meets a TUNING issue - a configuration or parameter whose
adjustment makes the system work better - it may fix it and continue, without returning for a
decision each time.

HOW THE BUILDER READS "TUNING", AND WHERE IT STOPS

Tuning means the values a system is meant to be adjusted by: generation parameters, thinking
flags, token budgets, timeouts, concurrency, retry counts. Things where the right value is an
empirical question and getting it wrong costs performance.

It is NOT a grant to change what the system is ALLOWED to do. The builder will keep bringing
these back, because calling one of them "tuning" is exactly how a limit gets removed by
accident:

  - Anything that widens what may be treated as fact (the evidence-admission surface of
    ENTRY 033).
  - Anything that relaxes a refusal: the local-model ceiling, the remote-execution refusal,
    path containment, the honesty guard that rejects uncited answers.
  - Anything touching provider spend. Still a mandatory STOP under AGENTS.md section 13.
  - Anything that would make a test pass by lowering what it asserts.

The distinction the builder is applying: tuning changes how well the system does its job;
it never changes what the system is permitted to do. A parameter that only exists to make a
guard quieter is not a tuning parameter.

THE FIRST FIX UNDER THIS GRANT

Measured: DEEP ran the full pipeline (about 6 minutes per question, four 8B models swapped
through 8 GB of VRAM) and produced visibly better reasoning than the single-model route -
and every run was REJECTED and discarded with:

  member_2 returned a truncated visible answer (done_reason=length)

The slate disables thinking for exactly ONE model:

  member_0     qwen3:8b        think=False   <- explicitly disabled
  member_1     deepseek-r1:8b  think=None
  member_2     dolphin3:8b     think=None
  critic       dolphin3:8b
  synthesizer  deepseek-r1:8b
  verifier     granite4.2:8b

think_by_model was {"qwen3:8b": false} and minimum_num_predict_by_model {"qwen3:8b": 1024}.
deepseek-r1 is ALSO a reasoning model - measured directly earlier in this session, it spent
2,985 characters thinking before answering and hit done_reason=length - so a member exhausts
its budget mid-answer and the pipeline correctly refuses to synthesise from a truncated
input.

The guard is right. The configuration was written for one model and never extended.

This is tuning by the definition above: no refusal is relaxed, nothing new becomes citeable,
and the honesty guard that rejected these runs stays exactly as it is. The fix makes the
models produce a complete visible answer so the guard has something valid to accept.

